<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-polynomial-approximations-of-differentiable-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Polynomial Approximations of Differentiable Functions
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now arrive at one of the fundamental results in differential calculus. <term> Taylor's theorem </term> establishes a best possible polynomial approximation <m> p </m> for a multiply differentiable function <m> f </m> near a given point. We quantify the quality of this approximation by asymptotically comparing the difference <m> f - p </m> to well-known power functions vanishing at <m> c </m> at high order.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-taylors-theorem">
    <title>
      Taylor's theorem
    </title>
    
    <creator>
      Brook Taylor
    </creator>
    
    <idx>
      Taylor's theorem
    </idx>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon X \to \R </m> be a real-valued function defined on a subset <m> X \subseteq \R </m>. If <m> f </m> is <m> k </m>-times differentiable at a point <m> c \in X </m>, then there is a unique polynomial function <m> p </m> of degree at most <m> k </m> so that <m> f \of{ x } - p \of{ x } \prec \of{ x - c }^{ k } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        For uniqueness, let <m> p , q \colon \R \to \R </m> be polynomial functions of degree at most <m> k </m>, and suppose that <m> f \of{ x } - p \of{ x } \prec \of{ x - c }^{ k } </m> and <m> f \of{ x } - q \of{ x } \prec \of{ x - c }^{ k } </m>. Then
        
        <md>
          0 \leq \abs{ \frac{ p \of{ x } - q \of{ x } }{ \of{ x - c }^{ k } } } \leq \abs{ \frac{ f \of{ x } - p \of{ x } }{ \of{ x - c }^{ k } } } + \abs{ \frac{ f \of{ x } - q \of{ x } }{ \of{ x - c }^{ k } } }
        </md>
        
        for all <m> x \in X </m>. The <xref ref="theorem-squeeze-theorem"/> now implies that
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ x \to c }{ \abs{ \frac{ p \of{ x } - q \of{ x } }{ \of{ x - c }^{ k } } } } = 0
        </md>,
        
        so that <m> p \of{ x } - q \of{ x } \prec \of{ x - c }^{ k } </m> as <m> x \to c </m>. This polynomial <m> p \of{ x } - q \of{ x } </m> can be written in the form
        
        <md>
          p \of{ x } - q \of{ x } = a_{ 0 } + a_{ 1 } \of{ x - c } + a_{ 2 } \of{ x - c }^{ 2 } + \dotsb + a_{ k } \of{ x - c }^{ k }
        </md>
        
        for some real numbers <m> a_{ 0 } , \dotsc , a_{ k } \in \R </m>. For each index <m> 0 \leq j \leq k </m>, we observe that
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ x \to c }{ \abs{ \frac{ p \of{ x } - q \of{ x } }{ \of{ x - c }^{ j } } } } = \of{ \lim_{ x \to c }{ \abs{ \frac{ p \of{ x } - q \of{ x } }{ \of{ x - c }^{ k } } } } } \of{ \lim_{ x \to c }{ \abs{ \of{ x - c }^{ k - j } } } } = 0 \cdot 0 = 0
        </md>.
        
        If <m> j = 0 </m>, then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            0 \amp = \lim_{ x \to c }{ \abs{ \frac{ p \of{ x } - q \of{ x } }{ \of{ x - c }^{ 0 } } } } = \lim_{ x \to c }{ \abs{ p \of{ x } - q \of{ x } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \lim_{ x \to c }{ \abs{ a_{ 0 } + a_{ 1 } \of{ x - c } + a_{ 2 } \of{ x - c }^{ 2 } + \dotsb + a_{ k } \of{ x - c }^{ k } } } = \abs{ a_{ 0 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        If <m> j = 1 </m>, then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            0 \amp = \lim_{ x \to c }{ \abs{ \frac{ p \of{ x } - q \of{ x } }{ \of{ x - c }^{ 1 } } } } = \lim_{ x \to c }{ \abs{ \frac{ p \of{ x } - q \of{ x } }{ x - c } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \lim_{ x \to c }{ \abs{ a_{ 1 } + a_{ 2 } \of{ x - c }^{ 1 } + \dotsb + a_{ k } \of{ x - c }^{ k - 1 } } } = \abs{ a_{ 1 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Continuing in this manner, we conclude that <m> a_{ 0 } = a_{ 1 } = \dotsb = a_{ k } = 0 </m>, so that <m> p = q </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        For existence, let <m> p \colon \R \to \R </m> be the polynomial function defined by the formula
        
        <md>
          p \of{ x } = f \of{ c } + f' \of{ c } \of{ x - c } + \frac{ f'' \of{ c } \of{ x - c }^{ 2 } }{ 2 } + \dotsb + \frac{ f^{ \of{ k } } \of{ c } \of{ x - c }^{ k } }{ k! }
        </md>.
        
        We observe that <m> p^{ \of{ j } } \of{ c } = f^{ \of{ j } } \of{ c } </m> for all <m> 0 \leq j \leq c </m>. We will show that <m> f \of{ x } - p \of{ x } \prec \of{ x - c }^{ k } </m> by repeated applications of <xref ref="corollary-lhopitals-rule" text="title"/>. Indeed,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \lim_{ x \to c }{ \frac{ f \of{ x } - p \of{ x } }{ \of{ x - c }^{ k } } } \amp = \lim_{ x \to c }{ \frac{ f' \of{ x } - p' \of{ x } }{ k \of{ x - c }^{ k - 1 } } } = \lim_{ x \to c }{ \frac{ f'' \of{ x } - p'' \of{ x } }{ k \of{ k - 1 } \of{ x - c }^{ k - 2 } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \dotsb = \lim_{ x \to c }{ \frac{ f^{ \of{ k - 1 } } \of{ x } - p^{ \of{ k - 1 } } \of{ x } }{ k! \of{ x - c } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ k! } } \of{ \lim_{ x \to c }{ \frac{ f^{ \of{ k - 1 } } \of{ x } - f^{ \of{ k - 1 } } \of{ c } }{ x - c } } - \lim_{ x \to c }{ \frac{ p^{ \of{ k - 1 } } \of{ x } - p^{ \of{ k - 1 } } \of{ c } }{ x - c } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \frac{ f^{ \of{ k } } \of{ c } - p^{ \of{ k } } \of{ c } }{ k! } = \frac{ 0 }{ k! } = 0
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        so that <m> f \of{ x } - p \of{ x } \prec \of{ x - c }^{ k } </m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  
  <p>
    In summary, if a real-valued function <m> f </m> is <m> k </m>-times differentiable at a point <m> c </m>, then the unique polynomial of degree at most <m> k </m> with the same value, and derivatives as <m> f </m> up to order <m> k </m> is the best possible polynomial approximation of <m> f </m> near <m> c </m>. This polynomial is called the <term> Taylor approximation </term> of <m> f </m> at <m> c </m>.
  </p>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-taylor-approximation-remainder">
    <title>
      Taylor approximation/remainder
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      Taylor approximation
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      Taylor remainder
    </idx>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> P^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the order <m> k </m> Taylor approximation of <m> f </m> at <m> c </m>
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> R^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the order <m> k </m> Taylor remainder of <m> f </m> at <m> c </m>
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon X \to \R </m> be a real-valued function defined on a subset <m> X \subseteq \R </m>. If <m> f </m> is <m> k </m>-times differentiable at a point <m> c \in X </m>, then the unique polynomial <m> P^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } </m> of degree at most <m> k </m> so that <m> f \of{ x } - P^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } \prec \of{ x - c }^{ k } </m> is called the order <m> k </m> <term> Taylor approximation </term> of <m> f </m> at <m> c </m>, and <m> R^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } = f \of{ x } - P^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } </m> is called the order <m> k </m> <term> Taylor remainder </term> of <m> f </m> at <m> c </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <p>
    The content of Taylor's theorem is not in finding the Taylor approximation, but rather in bounding the Taylor remainder. Under stronger hypotheses on <m> f </m>, one can find explicit formulas for the Taylor remainder. For example, the following result utilizes <xref ref="theorem-extended-mean-value-theorem"/> to compute a family of such an explicit formulas.
  </p>
  
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-mean-value-form-of-the-taylor-remainder">
    <title>
      Mean value form of the Taylor remainder
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon X \to \R </m> be a real-valued function defined on an interval <m> X \subseteq \R </m>, and suppose that <m> f </m> is <m> k </m>-times differentiable at an interior point <m> c \in \Int \of{ X } </m>, with order <m> k </m> Taylor approximation <m> P^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } </m> and order <m> k </m> Taylor remainder <m> R^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Fix a point <m> x \in X </m>, and a real-valued function <m> G \colon I_{ x } \to \R </m> defined on the closed interval <m> I_{ x } </m> between <m> c </m> and <m> x </m>. If both <m> f^{ \of{ k } } </m> and <m> G </m> are continuous on <m> I_{ x } </m> and differentiable on the open interval <m> \Int \of{ I_{ x } } </m>, and if <m> G </m> has no critical points on <m> \Int \of{ I_{ x } } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          R^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } = \of{ \frac{ f^{ \of{ k + 1 } } \of{ y } \of{ x - y }^{ k } }{ k! } } \of{ \frac{ G \of{ x } - G \of{ c } }{ G' \of{ y } } } 
        </md>
        
        for some interior point <m> y \in \Int \of{ I_{ x } } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Consider the real-valued function <m> F \colon X \to \R </m> defined by the formula
        
        <md>
          F \of{ y } = f \of{ y } + f' \of{ y } \of{ x - y } + \frac{ f'' \of{ y } \of{ x - y }^{ 2 } }{ 2 } + \dotsb + \frac{ f^{ \of{ k } } \of{ y } \of{ x - y }^{ k } }{ k! }
        </md>.
        
        We observe that <m> F </m> is continuous on the closed interval <m> I_{ x } </m> and differentiable on the open interval <m> \Int \of{ I_{ x } } </m>, with derivative
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            F' \of{ y } \amp = f' \of{ y } + \of{ f'' \of{ y } \of{ x - y } - f' \of{ y } } + \of{ \frac{ f^{ \of{ 3 } } \of{ y } \of{ x - y }^{ 2 } }{ 2 } - f'' \of{ y } \of{ x - y } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \qquad + \dotsb + \of{ \frac{ f^{ \of{ k + 1 } } \of{ y } \of{ x - y }^{ k } }{ k! } - \frac{ f^{ \of{ k } } \of{ y } \of{ x - y }^{ k - 1 } }{ \of{ k - 1 }! } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \frac{ f^{ \of{ k + 1 } } \of{ y } \of{ x - y }^{ k } }{ k! }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        By the <xref ref="theorem-extended-mean-value-theorem"/>, there is some interior point <m> y \in \Int \of{ I_{ x } } </m> so that
        
        <md>
          F' \of{ y } \of{ G \of{ x } - G \of{ c } } = \of{ F \of{ x } - F \of{ c } } G' \of{ y }
        </md>.
        
        We observe that <m> F \of{ x } = f \of{ x } </m> and <m> F \of{ c } = P^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } </m>, so that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            R^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } \amp = f \of{ x } - P^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } = F \of{ x } - F \of{ c }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = F' \of{ y } \of{ \frac{ G \of{ x } - G \of{ c } }{ G' \of{ y } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ \frac{ f^{ \of{ k + 1 } } \of{ y } \of{ x - y }^{ k } }{ k! } } \of{ \frac{ G \of{ x } - G \of{ c } }{ G' \of{ y } } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  
  <p>
    The utility of <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-form-of-the-taylor-remainder"/> is in choosing specific functions <m> G </m> in order to obtain particularly nice explicit formulas for the Taylor remainder. For example, we obtain the <term> Lagrange form </term> of the Taylor remainder, which is a higher order generalization of the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/>.
  </p>
  
  <corollary xml:id="corollary-lagrange-form-of-the-taylor-remainder">
    <title>
      Lagrange form of the Taylor remainder
    </title>
    
    <creator>
      Joseph-Louis Lagrange
    </creator>
    
    <idx>
      Lagrange form of the Taylor remainder
    </idx>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon X \to \R </m> be a real-valued function defined on an interval <m> X \subseteq \R </m>, and suppose that <m> f </m> is <m> k </m>-times differentiable at an interior point <m> c \in \Int \of{ X } </m>, with order <m> k </m> Taylor approximation <m> P^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } </m> and order <m> k </m> Taylor remainder <m> R^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Fix a point <m> x \in X </m>. If <m> f^{ \of{ k } } </m> is continuous on the closed interval <m> I_{ x } </m> between <m> c </m> and <m> x </m> and differentiable on the open interval <m> \Int \of{ I_{ x } } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          R^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } = \frac{ f^{ \of{ k + 1 } } \of{ y } \of{ x - c }^{ k + 1 } }{ \of{ k + 1 }! }
        </md>
        
        for some interior point <m> y \in \Int \of{ I_{ x } } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        This follows immediately from <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-form-of-the-taylor-remainder"/> by taking <m> G \of{ y } = \of{ x - y }^{ k + 1 } </m>. Indeed, in this case,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            R^{ k }_{ c } \of{ f , x } \amp = \of{ \frac{ f^{ \of{ k + 1 } } \of{ y } \of{ x - y }^{ k } }{ k! } } \of{ \frac{ G \of{ x } - G \of{ c } }{ G' \of{ y } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ \frac{ f^{ \of{ k + 1 } } \of{ y } \of{ x - y }^{ k } }{ k! } } \of{ \frac{ - \of{ x - c }^{ k + 1 } }{ \of{ k + 1 } \of{ x - y }^{ k } \of{ -1 } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \frac{ f^{ \of{ k + 1 } } \of{ y } \of{ x - c }^{ k + 1 } }{ \of{ k + 1 }! }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for some interior point <m> y \in \Int \of{ I_{ x } } </m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </corollary>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      We observe that <xref ref="corollary-lagrange-form-of-the-taylor-remainder"/> is precisely the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/> when <m> k = 0 </m>. Other important forms of the Taylor remainder can be found by choosing different functions <m> G </m>. You will have an opportunity to explore the <term> Cauchy form </term> of the Taylor remainder in the problems for this section.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</subsection>
